<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finance</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Remember that if you invest <m>P</m> dollars at an annual interest rate <m>r</m>
      compounded continuously, then after <m>T</m> years, you will have
      <md>
        Pe^{rT}\hbox{ dollars.}
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="0.3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that you invest $1000 at 7%.  How much will you have
        after 10 years?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20">
      <p>
        We'd have
        <md>
          P = 1000e^{0.07(10)} = 2103
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        After you successfully finish Math 202, you get a job computing
        volumes all day, and you are able to save $10,000 a year (this is
        called an <em>income stream</em>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose that you will retire in 30 years, continually saving
        $10,000 a year and investing it in an account at an annual interest
        rate of 7%.  We would like to determine how much money you will
        have at the end of 30 years.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shown below is a representation of the 30-year interval during which
        you will be working.  Time <m>t</m> represents the amount of
        time since you
        start working.
      </p>
      <image width="50%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="20-timeline">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/20-timeline.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much money would you save (i.e. put into the account)
          during a 10-year interval?   During a 2-year interval?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          We save $10,000 per year so in 10 years, we would save
          $100,000.  In 2 years, we would save $20,000.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the timeline above, a time interval of length <m>dt</m>
          is shown.  How
          much money do you save during this time interval of length
          <m>dt</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>dP=10000~dt</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you deposit this money <m>t</m> years after you start working, how
          long is it in the account before your retirement?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>30-t</m> years.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is its value when you retire?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>dV=1000e^{0.07(30-t)}~dt</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the total amount of money that you have when you
          retire?  This is called the <em>future value</em> of the
          income stream.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              FV \amp = \int_0^{30}1000e^{0.07(30-t)}~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -\frac{10000}{0.07}e^{0.07(30-t)}\biggl|_0^{30}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = 1,024,000
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the day you begin working, you learn that you've won the
          lottery.  Your first thought is to quit work and invest the
          winnings at 7%.  How much would you need to have won so
          that, after 30 years, you have the same amount you would
          have had if you worked and saved?  This is called the
          <em>present value</em> of the income stream.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>Pe^{0.07\cdot30} = 1024000</m> so <m>P=125,363</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let's imagine a different scenario: the longer you work, the
        more money you make and hence the more money you save.  For
        instance, suppose that you save at a rate of <m>P(t) =
        10,000+1000t</m> dollars per year.  How much will you have
        when you retire after 30 years?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20">
      <p>
        <md>
          \int_0^{30}(10000+1000t)e^{0.07(30-t)}~dt = 2,058,000
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If you would like to retire after 25 years with one million
        dollars, at what constant rate should you save money?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20">
      <p>
        Call <m>R</m> the savings rate so that
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^{25}Re^{0.07(25-t)}~dt \amp = 1000000
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            R \int_0^{25}e^{0.07(25-t)}~dt \amp = 1000000
          </mrow>
        </md>
        so <m>R=$14,772</m> per year.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that you save $5000 per year and you want to retire with
        one million dollars.  How long do you need to work?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="20">
      <p>
        Let <m>T</m> be the amount of time that you work so that
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^T5000e^{0.07(T-t)}~dt \amp = 1000000
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -\frac{5000}{0.07}e^{0.07(T-t)}\biggl|_0^T \amp = 1000000
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -\frac{5000}{0.07}(1-e^{0.07T}) \amp = 1000000
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            1-e^{0.07T} \amp = -14 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            T \amp = \frac{\ln(15)}{0.07} = 38.2
          </mrow>
        </md>
        years.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that in your work for an oil company, you discover an
        oil field.  Initially, it is easy to take oil from the field, but it
        becomes more difficult over time.  Therefore, the rate at which you
        can extract oil is
        <md>
          r(t) = 10 - 0.5t
        </md>
        millions of barrels per year.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take to remove all the oil?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>r(t)=0=10-0.5t</m> so <m>t=20</m> years.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How much oil is there in the field?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_0^{20}(10-0.5t)~dt = 100
          </md>
          million barrels.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming that the price of oil stays constant at $50 per barrel and
          an annual investment interest rate of 8%, what is the future value
          of the oil field?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_0^{20}50(10-0.5t)e^{0.08(20-t)}~dt = 15.5
          </md>
          billion dollars.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose you want to sell the oil field.  Why would you want to know
          the present value of the field?  What is the present value?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="20">
        <p>
          <m>Pe^{0.08\cdot20} = 15.5</m> so <m>P=3.13</m> billion.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
